# Node.js Gherkin E2E Tests

How to translate a shared Gherkin feature (`tests/definitions/**/*.feature`) into a
Node.js e2e test file (`nodejs/tests/e2e/**/*.test.ts`), and how to tag the feature
for the Node.js driver. Applies whenever you implement, edit, or tag a scenario the
Node.js e2e suite mirrors. Test-name casing is owned by `nodejs-test-standards` and
comment minimalism by `comment-prose`; this rule adds only the Gherkin-specific parts.

## Feature → test-file structure

- One shared `Connection` per top-level `describe` block: connect in that block's
  `beforeAll`, destroy in its `afterAll`. This matches the rest of the suite and keeps
  the run fast.
- The feature's scenarios live in a `describe` named after the **repo-relative feature
  path** — `describe('tests/definitions/shared/types/<name>.feature')`. That path is
  the traceability link, so nothing above it restates it in a header comment.
- One `it()` per implemented scenario; the name copies the scenario name verbatim
  (already `should`-prefixed — see `nodejs-test-standards`).
- Tests with **no** matching scenario (driver-specific options, APIs the feature does
  not cover) sit **outside** the feature `describe`, grouped in a `describe` named
  after the option or API they exercise. Sitting outside already says the feature does
  not cover them, so no comment says it again.
- A fixture used by a **single** test (e.g. a temp table) is created in the test body
  and cleaned up in `try`/`finally`. Wrapping one `it()` in a `describe` just to get
  `beforeAll`/`afterAll` splits the scenario across blocks and buries the
  `And ... is created` step. A nested `describe` + hooks is for a fixture several tests
  share.

## Gherkin step comments (the traceability link)

- Copy only the **step lines** — `Given` / `When` / `Then` / `And` — verbatim, and put
  real code under every one. No empty steps; this is enforced by
  `ud-no-empty-gherkin-step-implementations`.
- The `Given Snowflake client is logged in` step is the exception with no code of its
  own — the shared `Connection` is opened once in `beforeAll`. Put `void connection;`
  under it, followed by a blank line, so the step is not empty. Without it the validator
  reads the next step's comment as the first line under `Given` and reports the step
  empty.

```ts
  // Given Snowflake client is logged in
  void connection;

  // When Query "SELECT ..." is executed
```
- Do **not** copy a step's **data table** into the comments. `tests_format_validator`
  drops table rows when it parses the feature (`feature_parser.rs`) but folds any
  comment line following a step comment into that step's text (`step_finder.rs`), so a
  copied `//   | col1 | col2 |` turns `// Then the result should contain:` into a step
  the validator cannot match, and the scenario reports as unimplemented. The `expect`
  carries those values anyway.
- Do **not** copy the feature file's `#` lines: those are the author's notes, not part
  of the scenario, so importing them pins the test to prose nothing checks. Where a `#`
  block is the real specification of a vague step (an enumerated corner-case list under
  a single `When`), honor it in code — one entry per bullet — not in copied prose.
- These step comments are the one exemption to `comment-prose`'s minimalism: they are
  the link back to the scenario, not narration. Their data tables are not exempt — omit
  them, as above. Everything else in the file follows `comment-prose`.
- Pass SQL **inline** to the execute helper. A `const query = …` that restates the
  `// When` comment only drifts from it.

```ts
it('should <scenario name copied verbatim>', async () => {
  // Given Snowflake client is logged in
  void connection;

  // When Query "SELECT ..." is executed
  const { rows } = await executeAsync(connection, `SELECT ...`);
  // Then the result should contain the expected values
  expect(Object.values(rows[0])).toEqual([/* ... */]);
});
```

## Scenario disposition (which bucket a scenario lands in)

Every scenario the feature marks for Node maps to exactly one of these. Pick the row,
then follow the detailed section below.

| Situation | Test form | `@nodejs_e2e` on the scenario | Difference recorded in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Runs on both drivers | `it(...)` | yes | — |
| New driver cannot do it yet | `describe`/`it.skipIf(NOT_IMPLEMENTED_IN_NEW_DRIVER)` | no | — |
| Drivers differ by design | `it(...)` + `isRunningNewDriverWithBD('BD#N')` | yes | `nodejs/BehaviorDifferences.yaml` |
| Broken in both drivers (bug, no fix yet) | `it.todo(...)`, step comments kept | yes | `nodejs/BCR_LOG.md` |

The last row keeps the `// Given` / `// When` / `// Then` comments in the `it.todo` body
so scenario matching still resolves it (matching keys off the step comments, above); the
`todo` marks it claimed-but-not-yet-passing. Log the bug in `nodejs/BCR_LOG.md` rather
than writing an assertion that passes on the wrong value.

## Feature tags

- Add `@nodejs` to the `Feature:` line and `@nodejs_e2e` to exactly the scenarios the
  **new** driver runs — not the skip-for-new ones. Mirror how sibling features in the
  same directory are tagged.
- This satisfies `ud-dt-gherkin-driver-tags-match-claimed-support`: feature-level
  `@nodejs` for a type the driver ships, scenario-level `@nodejs_e2e` on what it
  actually runs. When Node covers a scenario only through a driver-specific test, the
  choice is either to tag the feature and implement the step comments, or to omit the
  tag and record the gap in `nodejs/BehaviorDifferences.yaml`.

## Skip policy — old-driver-green is the bar

- Scenarios the new driver cannot yet satisfy (typically parameter binding and
  multi-chunk download, plus anything with an open gap) are wrapped in
  `describe.skipIf(NOT_IMPLEMENTED_IN_NEW_DRIVER)` (or `it.skipIf(...)`). `skipIf`
  already states the new driver cannot run them, so no comment restates that.
- A scenario broken in **both** drivers is `it.todo`, not `skipIf` (which runs under the
  old driver and would then fail) — see the disposition table.
- Those blocks still run and must pass under `npm run test:e2e-old-driver`; they are
  skipped under `npm run test:e2e`. Everything else runs on both drivers.

## Behavior differences

When the old and new drivers legitimately differ, branch on
`isRunningNewDriverWithBD('BD#N')` and make sure `BD#N` exists in
`nodejs/BehaviorDifferences.yaml` (add an entry when the difference is new). Asserting
only the old-driver value hides the difference. Cite `BD#N` only where the code reads
`isRunningNewDriverWithBD('BD#N')`; when both drivers agree, the registry entry carries
the cross-driver story, not a comment.

A *bug* neither driver gets right is not a `BD#N`: mark that scenario `it.todo` and log
it in `nodejs/BCR_LOG.md` (disposition table), rather than adding a difference entry for
behavior no driver has decided on yet.

## SQL safety & determinism

- Values reach the server as `binds`, not string interpolation — except same-file
  constant literals and `randomizeName()` identifiers, which are exempt.
- Any table or multi-row assertion is deterministic: add an `ID` column and
  `ORDER BY ID` rather than relying on natural order.
- Temp tables are created as
  `CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY TABLE ${randomizeName('<PREFIX>')} (...)` and
  `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS`-ed in the owning test's `finally` (or in `afterAll` when the
  table is shared by several tests).

```ts
const tableName = randomizeName('<PREFIX>');
await executeAsync(connection, `CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY TABLE ${tableName} (...)`);
try {
  // populate, query, assert
} finally {
  await executeAsync(connection, `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS ${tableName}`);
}
```

## Assertions restate the expected result, not the fixture

A value used by exactly one test belongs in that test — inline in `it.each([...])`, in
the `expect`, or a local `const`. A file-level constant is for a table genuinely reused
across tests. Building a query and its expectation by mapping over the same array makes
the assertion restate the fixture instead of the expected result, so a reader has to
run the `map` in their head to see what the test proves. Write the literals in the SQL
and the same literals in the `expect`.

## Verify

From `nodejs/`:

```bash
npm run test:e2e-old-driver -- <file>.test.ts   # the bar: must pass, incl. skip-for-new blocks
npm run test:e2e -- <file>.test.ts              # non-skipped scenarios pass on the new driver
```

`test:e2e-old-driver` sets `SNOWFLAKE_NODEJS_E2E_USE_OLD_DRIVER=1`, which makes
`NOT_IMPLEMENTED_IN_NEW_DRIVER === false`, so the skip-for-new blocks actually run.

